# Security and caching headers for Circulation Studio
# See DEPLOYMENT.md for detailed configuration
#
# Paths here must match the paths the site is SERVED at, not the source tree.
# Vite fingerprints every asset into /assets/, so the previous /css/*, /js/*,
# /images/* and /fonts/* rules matched nothing at all.
#
# IMPORTANT, this is why Cache-Control is not on /* below:
# Cloudflare Pages does NOT resolve duplicate headers by specificity. When two
# matching rules set the same header, it JOINS the values with a comma. A
# Cache-Control on /* plus another on /assets/* would ship
# "public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
# on every asset, which is ambiguous. So any given Cache-Control must be set by
# exactly one matching rule, and these path rules must never overlap for it.

# Global security headers. Deliberately no Cache-Control here, see above.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()

# Build output: CSS, JS and fonts, all emitted by Vite with a content hash in
# the filename. Safe to cache forever, because the filename changes whenever
# the bytes do. This is the only rule setting Cache-Control on these paths.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Service worker (if PWA module enabled). Unused today, PWA is No in
# SITE_ARCHITECTURE.md. Kept because a service worker must never be cached.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# HTML pages intentionally carry no Cache-Control rule. Pretty URLs mean the
# request path is "/" or "/services/", so the only glob that would catch them
# is /*, which also catches /assets/ and would corrupt the asset value through
# the comma-join above. Pages therefore fall back to the Cloudflare Pages
# default for HTML, which revalidates rather than caching hard. That is the
# safe posture: content edits go live immediately, and the fingerprinted assets
# they reference are still cached for a year. If an explicit page max-age is
# ever wanted, it has to be added as non-overlapping rules per page path, not
# as a second /* rule.
